{
  "teamId": 144,
  "year": 2020,
  "narrative": "The 2020 Atlanta Braves arrived in a shortened season full of promise and delivered on it in emphatic fashion, posting a 35-25 record and finishing as one of the National League's most dangerous clubs. From the very first week, the offense announced itself loudly — Marcell Ozuna setting the tone with a home run on July 25th, and Travis d'Arnaud delivering a massive two-run double just days later. The Braves were built to hit, and their 103 home runs and .832 OPS proved it all summer long.\n\nAugust became the team's defining month, a stretch where the roster's depth and big-game DNA showed up repeatedly. Dansby Swanson's walk-off two-run blast on August 17th — a 0.893 WPA swing, the highest of the entire season — encapsulated what this team was capable of: delivering when it mattered most. Freddie Freeman, in the middle of an MVP campaign, added his own fireworks, while role players like Matt Adams and Adam Duvall chipped in clutch extra-base hits. Even a late-August collapse by Mark Melancon couldn't derail what had become a genuine NL contender.\n\nSeptember brought continued winning, a final-weekend explosion against the Red Sox — with Ozuna, Freeman, and Swanson all delivering in the same series — and a sense that Atlanta was genuinely built for October. The Wild Card and NLDS passed without major drama, the Braves dispatching the Reds with relative ease and leaning on young talent like Ian Anderson and veterans like Travis d'Arnaud, whose three-run homer in the Division Series captured the team's clutch identity.\n\nBut the NLCS against the Los Angeles Dodgers told a different story. Atlanta took an early lead, with Austin Riley homering in Game 1, yet the Dodgers proved relentless. Will Smith's three-run homer on October 16th shifted the series' momentum decisively, and by Game 7, Ian Anderson and the bullpen crumbled under Dodger pressure — Will Smith singling in two, Enrique Hernandez and Cody Bellinger adding gut-punch home runs. The dream of a World Series ended in the bubble, but the foundation of something special had been unmistakably laid.",
  "chapters": [
